\f0\fs22 \cf2 In the paperwork it was a flow event.\
There had been eleven gates and four of them were staffed, because staffing all eleven had been costed at an amount the operator described, in a submission later read aloud at the inquiry to general agreement, as difficult to justify against projected attendance. Projected attendance had been calculated by the same department. Everybody funnelled to the four gates that worked. The four gates that worked were fitted with barriers designed to prevent people entering the wrong way, which they did, superbly, for the entire duration.\
The inquiry found that the deceased had failed to disperse in an orderly manner. It recommended better signage.\
What nobody had put in a submission, because nobody in an office had ever needed to know it, was that a crowd pressed hard enough does not fall over. There is nowhere to fall. It stands. And when it stops, it goes on standing, and some hours later it sets, and by the time anyone with the authority to sign anything arrives at the site, what is waiting for them is not a heap. It is a wall, and the wall is made of people, and they are all still on their feet.\
Chanta was in the fourth crew in.\
The first three had tried it properly. Two-man teams, gloves, straps, a chaplain who had been rung at four in the morning and had come. They worked from the outside in for six hours and got eleven out, which they laid down on the tarpaulin in the order they came, and which the chaplain named, and which was, everybody agreed, the right way to do it.\
The problem was arithmetic. Eleven out of what the operator\u8217 ?s own turnstile data insisted was four thousand two hundred, over six hours, is a job that finishes in about eleven weeks, and there was a Sunday market booked.\
So at half past two a man in a hard hat with a clipboard walked the length of it, and made a phone call, and then made a second phone call to somebody senior enough to say the thing out loud, and at ten past three a front-end loader came up the ramp.\
Chanta\u8217 ?s crew were told to stand back from the plant and work behind it.\
That was the whole of the briefing. Behind it.\
He got through most of the afternoon by not looking up. This was a technique he had, and he was better at it than anyone else on his crew, and it was the only thing in his life he had ever been told he had a gift for. You worked a square metre. You did not look at the square metre next to yours until it was your square metre. You did not look at the loader, and you did not look at where the loader had been, and above all you did not look at the front of it, which had a shape now that it had not had at ten past three.\
At about five he stopped being able to do it.\
He got himself behind the wall of the substation at the western end, where the light was bad and the smell was mostly diesel, and put a hand on the brickwork, and was sick until there was nothing left, and then a while longer.\
When he opened his eyes there was a policeman on his knees a metre away, in the good spot, doing exactly the same thing.\
Chanta had not heard him arrive, because you don\u8217 ?t.\
The policeman was young. His uniform had never been worn; it still had the packing creases across the back and the hard edge on the collar, and the boots were a colour boots stop being in about a week. There was a rank on the shoulder. Chanta had cleaned up after the police for years and had never seen that rank on anybody, and had a vague sense that if he asked about it he would be given an answer, at length.\
Neither of them said anything for a while. It seemed fair.\
\u8220 ?There\u8217 ?s a bin,\u8221 ? Chanta offered eventually, and nodded at it, and then realised the bin was full and had been since about two, and let it go.\
The policeman waved a hand. It was a soft hand. His face was round and his eyes were small and pink at the edges and his ears had gone a colour, and up close there was something about the whole arrangement that made Chanta think, without any unkindness and without pursuing it, of an animal in a tree.\
\u8220 ?I\u8217 ?m not supposed to be back here,\u8221 ? the policeman said.\
\u8220 ?Nobody\u8217 ?s supposed to be back here.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?No, I mean I\u8217 ?m required to be at the front. There\u8217 ?s a \u8212 ?\u8221 ? He stopped and swallowed and got it under control. \u8220 ?There\u8217 ?s a photograph at six.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Of what?\u8221 ?\
The policeman looked at him.\
\u8220 ?Right,\u8221 ? Chanta said.\
Chanta sat down on the concrete with his back against the substation wall, because his legs had made the decision without consulting him, and got out his sandwich. Clean-up crews were fed; it was in the award, and it was the only clause in the award that had never been amended.\
He had been carrying it since eleven and had not opened it. He opened it now, out of curiosity more than appetite, and it turned out that whoever catered a thing like this had decided to be generous about it.\
The policeman\u8217 ?s head came round.\
\u8220 ?Is that real beef?\u8221 ?\
Chanta looked at the sandwich. Then he held it out.\
\u8220 ?You can have it,\u8221 ? he said. \u8220 ?I don\u8217 ?t think I\u8217 ?ll need it.\u8221 ?\
The policeman took it in both hands. He ate the whole thing, sitting on his heels in the dark next to a substation, with his eyes shut, and he made a noise partway through that he was almost certainly not aware of.\
Somewhere out past the wall the loader changed gear.\
They both went very still.\
The thing about the wall was that it was two metres of brick and there was nothing to see. Chanta looked at it anyway. He kept looking at it, and he was aware, in the way you are aware of weather, that his chest had gone tight and stupid, and that he wanted very much to be somewhere with a door in it, and that the other bloke had stopped chewing.\
It went on for a bit.\
Then the loader finished whatever it was doing and idled, and the air came back into both of them at about the same time, and neither of them said anything about it, then or ever.\
The policeman stood up.\
Chanta watched him do it, and that was the part Chanta remembered, when he remembered any of it, which was not often.\
He stood up and he put his shoulders back, and he ran both hands down the front of his tunic, and then he did something with his face. It took under a second. Chanta had seen a woman in a karaoke bar do the same thing between the door and the microphone and it had taken her four steps; this took a second and a half, and it was better work.\
\u8220 ?Lieutenant Alexander Shakti,\u8221 ? said the policeman, and put out his hand, and his voice had come from somewhere else entirely. It was a beautiful voice. It sounded like the second half of a sentence you had already agreed with. \u8220 ?Public Relations. Well\u8212 ?Homicide, formally, but one wears several hats, my good fellow, one wears several hats.\u8221 ?\
Chanta looked at the hand for a moment and then shook it.\
\u8220 ?Chanta.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Chanta.\u8221 ? Alexander held on a fraction longer than the handshake needed. \u8220 ?And what is it you do here, Chanta?\u8221 ?\
Chanta gestured at his overalls. There was a logo over the breast pocket. It was upside down to Alexander and Alexander read it anyway, which meant he had already read it, before, at some point, and had asked the question anyway.\
\u8220 ?Ah,\u8221 ? Alexander said. \u8220 ?The very best sort of chap. The absolutely indispensable sort.\u8221 ? He produced a card from somewhere on his person and put it into Chanta\u8217 ?s hand, closing Chanta\u8217 ?s fingers over it with both of his own, which were still warm and did not feel like hands. \u8220 ?Now. Between us\u8212 ?and I do mean between us, Chanta\u8212 ?that is not a scene I would wish on a colleague. All right? Between us.\u8221 ?\
Chanta had not said anything to be between them.\
\u8220 ?Sure,\u8221 ? he said.\
\u8220 ?Good man. Good man.\u8221 ? Alexander was already turning towards the front, already several metres ahead of himself, already, Chanta could see, at the photograph. \u8220 ?If you\u8217 ?re ever in any trouble at all, you ring me. Any trouble. Straight to me.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?Right.\u8221 ?\
\u8220 ?I mean it, my old mate.\u8221 ? And he was gone round the corner of the substation, and half a minute later, out at the front, somebody with a good camera got a picture that ran on three networks of a young officer with his cap under his arm, looking off to the left of frame with an expression of enormous and carefully lit sorrow.\
Chanta stayed where he was until the light went.\
Then he went back and finished his square metre.}